## Example

Let $\Omega=\{\omega_+,\omega_-\}$, $\mathcal F=\mathcal P(\Omega)$, and $w(\omega_+)=3/4$, $w(\omega_-)=1/4$. Define $P(A)=\sum_{\omega\in A}w(\omega)$ for each $A\in\mathcal F$; then $(\Omega,\mathcal F,P)$ is a probability space. Let $Y:\Omega\to\mathbb R$ be the random variable $Y(\omega_+)=2a$ and $Y(\omega_-)=-2a$, where $a>0$ and the values are numerical values in the unit $\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. This finite model makes $Y$ measurable and integrable. The label $Y$ is only an illustrative entropy-valued variable; this example adopts no physical path law.

This arithmetic example illustrates the distinction in [[rem-td-fluctuation-model-hypotheses]].

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The two-outcome model and assignments stated above.

[F1] [[lem-expectation-by-distribution]]: for a real random variable on a finite probability space, $\mathbb E[Y]=\sum_{y\in Y(\Omega)}yP(Y=y)$.

[A1] The finite probability space and random variable are those stated in the Example: $w(\omega_+)+w(\omega_-)=1$, $Y(\omega_+)=2a$, $Y(\omega_-)=-2a$, and $a>0$.

## Verification

**Given:** [F1] and [A1].

1.1 The two values of $Y$ are distinct because $a>0$, so [F1] gives $\mathbb E[Y]=(3/4)(2a)+(1/4)(-2a)=a>0$. Its unit is $\mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}$. [F1, A1, algebra]

1.2 The event $\{\omega\in\Omega:Y(\omega)<0\}$ is exactly $\{\omega_-\}$, so $P(Y<0)=1/4>0$. [A1, algebra]

2.1 Steps 1.1 and 1.2 exhibit a positive mean together with a negative realization of nonzero probability. Therefore positivity of an ensemble mean alone does not imply a nonnegative value in every trial. The example is not a fluctuation theorem or a claim about measured entropy production. [step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
